WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.470 --> 00:00:07.509
National Geographic: Lithium, a soft, silver-gray
metal, is the third lightest element in the

00:00:07.509 --> 00:00:08.509
universe.

00:00:08.509 --> 00:00:11.510
Originally discovered in 1817 in a piece of
volcanic stone.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:15.340
PBS NewsHour: We’re standing on some lithium
now, right?

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.490
It’s there, you just can’t really tell
it on the surface.

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:23.920
WION: Beneath the desert lies a treasure,
the white gold, better known as lithium.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:24.920
SIERRA: Lithium.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.269
Atomic number three on the periodic table.

00:00:27.269 --> 00:00:30.930
It’s not one of the more famous elements,
like gold.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:36.660
But behind the scenes, as countries compete
to get ahead in the global energy transition,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.239
lithium has become one of the world’s most
valuable commodities.

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:41.280
Why?

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:46.070
Well it’s an essential ingredient in lithium-ion
batteries.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.670
Not glamorous, sure.

00:00:47.670 --> 00:00:53.109
But without those batteries there’s no way
to power an electric car, or store energy

00:00:53.109 --> 00:00:54.990
from wind and solar.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:01:00.879
As countries transition to a green future,
demand for lithium is exploding, and supply

00:01:00.879 --> 00:01:02.180
just can’t keep up.

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:07.430
The world is going to need a lot more of it,
and without it, the fight against climate

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.250
change could come to a grinding halt.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:14.420
I’m Gabrielle Sierra and this is Why It
Matters.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:21.830
Today, lithium and the future of energy.

00:01:21.830 --> 00:01:30.420
SIERRA: You know, I don't personally spend
a lot of time thinking about various metals.

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.409
So why should lithium matter to me or to any
of us?

00:01:34.409 --> 00:01:39.299
VAITHEESWARAN: So I also don't think about
metals all the time, but I do think an awful

00:01:39.299 --> 00:01:43.420
lot about my smartphone and I'll bet you lots
of our audience do as well.

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:49.079
SIERRA: This is Vijay Vaitheeswaran, the global
energy and climate innovation editor at The

00:01:49.079 --> 00:01:50.979
Economist Magazine.

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:56.560
VAITHEESWARAN: They probably also think a
lot about the many other gadgets in their

00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:57.640
lives.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:03.600
And the main battery source to power all of
our gadgets and gizmos in modern life, whether

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:10.979
it's the smartphones, to the camcorders, to
you name it, these days use lithium-ion batteries.

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:16.349
Lithium is particularly attractive to engineers
because we're able to get an enormous amount

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:18.050
of energy storage.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:22.640
You can pack 200 watt hours of electrical
potential into a single kilogram.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.990
That's a five times improvement on the old
lead acid batteries.

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:28.440
So in a sense, you get a lot a bang for the
buck.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:32.800
The costs have fallen by about 98% for the
energy stored in the last 30 years.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.800
So it's a very, very attractive way to store
a lot of energy in a small space to power

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:39.270
an electronic device.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:44.170
SIERRA: Batteries that use lithium are easy
to recharge and relatively cheap.

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:50.030
As a result, your home is probably full of
battery-operated objects that contain lithium.

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.660
But while smartphones and laptops and other
gizmos are great and all, the real reason

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:59.480
that lithium-powered batteries matter has
to do with something way bigger.

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:05.370
Clips: To address climate change, climate
change, change, climate change, climate change.

00:03:05.370 --> 00:03:06.580
SIERRA: Climate change.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.450
VAITHEESWARAN: It really comes back to this
question of energy storage, right?

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:15.980
It's not as glamorous as windmills or even
hydrogen, some people like to talk about.

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:20.190
Some people like to get into all manner of
new technologies for making electricity that

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:23.480
are green, but storing it is the other half
of the equation.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:26.379
It's kind of the ugly duckling of the clean
energy revolution.

00:03:26.379 --> 00:03:31.810
SIERRA: Lithium-powered batteries are fundamental
to storing and distributing clean energy.

00:03:31.810 --> 00:03:36.750
There may be alternatives in the distant future,
but right now, there is just no way to make

00:03:36.750 --> 00:03:38.421
clean energy work without it.

00:03:38.421 --> 00:03:44.180
And this impacts another sector of the energy
transition - electric vehicles.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:49.080
Ford Motor Company: It’s a driving experience
that’s pure, unfiltered exhilaration, from

00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.030
the moment you hit the accelerator.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.980
It’s the all-new F-150 Lightning.

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:03.250
SIERRA: According to the US Department of
Energy, highway vehicles release about 1.6

00:04:03.250 --> 00:04:08.049
billion tons of greenhouse gasses into the
atmosphere every year.

00:04:08.049 --> 00:04:15.300
At the global level, road transport accounts
for something like 15 percent of all C02 emissions.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:20.259
And that means electric vehicles have the
potential to take a huge bite out of the climate

00:04:20.259 --> 00:04:22.220
problem.

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:27.240
VAITHEESWARAN: Anybody who cares about climate
change will tell you, "Hey, we've got to get

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:28.540
off of oil."

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:33.270
And the most promising technology for getting
off of petroleum in transportation at least

00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:39.940
for the next 10 to 15 years is electrification
and electric cars rely on lithium-ion batteries.

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:42.980
There aren't really a lot of viable substitutes
in the short term.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:48.360
So yeah, we're going to demand a lot more
lithium if the electric revolution keeps taking

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.970
off, like it is, certainly in China, certainly
in Europe, to some degree in California and

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:55.610
parts of the US with the Teslas and so on.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.729
And that's going to consume ever more lithium
to make those batteries.

00:04:58.729 --> 00:05:06.880
SIERRA: An EV battery needs around 17-25 pounds
of refined lithium to run.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:13.270
That's enough to power up to 10,000 smartphones.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:20.480
SIERRA: So I live in New York and I have a
license, but I don't drive.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:25.200
So I kind of feel like I don't have a grasp
on the role they're playing in car sales.

00:05:25.200 --> 00:05:28.539
So currently in the US, how many car sales
are EVs?

00:05:28.539 --> 00:05:29.900
FANNON: Yeah.

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:33.790
EVs are a tiny portion of the overall vehicle
fleet.

00:05:33.790 --> 00:05:36.890
I mean, globally, it's not even double digits.

00:05:36.890 --> 00:05:39.389
SIERRA: This is Frank Fannon.

00:05:39.389 --> 00:05:44.389
Frank served as Assistant Secretary of State
for Energy Resources, and he is also Managing

00:05:44.389 --> 00:05:47.789
Director at Fannon Global Advisors.

00:05:47.789 --> 00:05:49.140
And he’s right.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:55.330
Despite the flashy commercials, EVs accounted
for just 3% of total sales in the U.S last

00:05:55.330 --> 00:05:56.330
year.

00:05:56.330 --> 00:05:58.900
But that hasn’t stopped leaders from setting
ambitious goals.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:03.039
Joe Biden/Guardian News: Now that’s why
today I’m signing an executive order setting

00:06:03.039 --> 00:06:10.830
out a target of 50% of all passenger vehicles
sold by 2030 will be electric.

00:06:10.830 --> 00:06:14.520
FANNON: There has to be an exponential growth
in demand.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:18.280
And one of the things that the automakers
are doing, is they're putting real money behind

00:06:18.280 --> 00:06:19.330
this, in this conversion.

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.880
I mean, some of these announcements that they're
going to exit the internal combustion engine

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:29.270
within the 10 to 15 years, depending on the
company, is an absolute tremendous scaled

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:30.509
sea change.

00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:35.449
SIERRA: So it sounds like they aren't a big
part of car sales yet, but car makers and

00:06:35.449 --> 00:06:40.600
governments are basically planning for them
to become the dominant model in the near future.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:41.880
FANNON: Absolutely.

00:06:41.880 --> 00:06:43.520
And this is a global phenomenon.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.880
And there's an appropriate amount of attention
here at home in the United States.

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:52.340
But the biggest by far market for electric
vehicles isn't the United States, it's China.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:59.139
SIERRA: Last year EV sales more than doubled
in China, with 2.9 million electric vehicles

00:06:59.139 --> 00:07:00.139
sold.

00:07:00.139 --> 00:07:02.919
That puts them way ahead of the United States.

00:07:02.919 --> 00:07:04.970
But the US is seeing momentum.

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:10.319
MSNBC: Tonight Ford just announced that they
are hiring 11,000 new employees.

00:07:10.319 --> 00:07:11.319
11,000 new jobs.

00:07:11.319 --> 00:07:14.500
Bloomberg Markets and Finance: They’re gonna
ramp up their spending, but to the tune now

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:17.960
of $20 billion dollars to quicken their EV
production.

00:07:17.960 --> 00:07:21.590
CBS Evening News: Ambitious declaration from
the CEO of General Motors tonight, the auto

00:07:21.590 --> 00:07:28.699
giant making plans to spend $35 billion dollars
on EVs over the next 4 years.

00:07:28.699 --> 00:07:33.980
SIERRA: All of these cars will need a lot
of lithium, and if we don’t have enough

00:07:33.980 --> 00:07:38.220
supply, it could halt the momentum.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:44.780
FANNON: Lithium is really bespoke to electric
vehicle technology and some other battery

00:07:44.780 --> 00:07:46.740
storage types of solutions.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:51.289
It's really instrumental to whether we can
achieve the targets.

00:07:51.289 --> 00:07:54.170
And there's billions of dollars being invested
in this.

00:07:54.170 --> 00:07:59.400
All of those investments are dependent on
a stable, secure supply of lithium.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:05.050
Right now demand is far exceeding supply by
something like three to one.

00:08:05.050 --> 00:08:08.050
I don't think we've reconciled this quite
yet.

00:08:08.050 --> 00:08:13.070
But just the scale of this demand-supply gap,
according to a firm out of London called Benchmark

00:08:13.070 --> 00:08:17.569
Minerals Intelligence, they've assessed that
in order to achieve the Glasgow Declaration

00:08:17.569 --> 00:08:25.229
by 2040, would require 17 times more battery
quality lithium to be developed than is produced

00:08:25.229 --> 00:08:26.520
last year in 2021.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:28.560
SIERRA: Did you get that?

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:32.479
17 times more lithium than the world currently
produces.

00:08:32.479 --> 00:08:37.280
FANNON: So how are we going to achieve this
is a huge, huge challenge.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:42.110
And the other challenge we have is the time
it takes to develop one of these projects.

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:46.550
You can't just go to the store, like you can
go to the store and buy a new phone or go

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:49.020
to the Tesla showroom and buy a car.

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:53.120
It takes a long lead-time to develop some
of these mining projects.

00:08:53.120 --> 00:08:55.710
And we are playing catch up.

00:08:55.710 --> 00:09:01.550
SIERRA: So I guess this is when I ask you,
how do we get lithium?

00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:03.210
VAITHEESWARAN: There's plenty of lithium.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:04.480
That's the good news.

00:09:04.480 --> 00:09:05.890
There's not enough lithium.

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:07.220
That's the bad news.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:12.400
A handful of countries lead the production
of lithium in the world.

00:09:12.400 --> 00:09:17.040
Bolivia probably has the biggest resources
in the ground and Chile and Argentina, its

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:20.040
neighbors, together they make up what's called
the Lithium Triangle.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:22.840
They have lots and lots of resources in the
ground.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:26.529
But Australia is actually the biggest producer
as a mining country.

00:09:26.529 --> 00:09:31.360
They know how to do it, and they make their
lithium available to the world quite economically.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:34.570
China has lithium that it produces, although
it's not one of the biggest producers.

00:09:34.570 --> 00:09:37.410
And so does the United States, although we're
not one of the big producers either.

00:09:37.410 --> 00:09:40.110
There are two main ways you get lithium.

00:09:40.110 --> 00:09:42.209
Lithium is mined out of a rock.

00:09:42.209 --> 00:09:45.579
That's the Australian approach, it's found
in deposits over there in that way.

00:09:45.579 --> 00:09:48.500
In South America, they get lithium in a different
way.

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:49.810
It's actually kind of cool.

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:52.019
They have these salt flats.

00:09:52.019 --> 00:09:54.630
These massive occurrences of lithium that
are underground.

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:59.980
So you drill through a layer of salt in order
to get the lithium out, and then you have

00:09:59.980 --> 00:10:04.079
to evaporate it in these giant pools of water,
for many months at a time.

00:10:04.079 --> 00:10:10.300
So it's slow, it's complicated, it's environmentally
messy, and it's not easily replicated in other

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:12.589
parts of the world or other places.

00:10:12.589 --> 00:10:19.420
FANNON: So you have the mining of the material,
but that's just a raw rock, or kind of dust

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:23.649
in a bag that provides no useful purpose at
all.

00:10:23.649 --> 00:10:28.870
Just like if you drill in West Texas and have
oil, it's not useful for anything.

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.300
You can't put that in your car and drive.

00:10:31.300 --> 00:10:34.070
So too is the raw commodity of lithium.

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:39.320
What you need to do is go forward and refine
it, process it, and go through these stages,

00:10:39.320 --> 00:10:43.120
till it meets a certain type of quality, that
then can be useful.

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:48.420
SIERRA: And while there is lithium all over
the world, this refinement process is largely

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:51.000
controlled by one country.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:59.050
FANNON: China controls more than 60% of the
global processing capacity for lithium.

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:04.310
That degree of concentration represents a
material volatility, potentially in the price,

00:11:04.310 --> 00:11:10.490
but also an oversized degree of control over
our collective clean energy future.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:14.560
China's focus on this is a decade in the making
plus.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.100
And they've been remarkably successful.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:20.910
China's focus on these clean energy technologies
is really one of security.

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:23.700
They want to control the commanding heights
of the new economy.

00:11:23.700 --> 00:11:27.060
And that is very much tied to the clean energy
economy.

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:31.390
VAITHEESWARAN: They have some lithium themselves,
but they're a huge importer from other countries

00:11:31.390 --> 00:11:33.890
like Australia and from the South American
countries.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:37.730
And the reason is they've built the supply
chain and the manufacturing processes.

00:11:37.730 --> 00:11:44.130
They have developed a process for making lithium-ion
batteries at scale, a manufacturing process

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:46.490
that consumes lithium as an input.

00:11:46.490 --> 00:11:48.380
And so they've got the electrochemical know-how.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:53.560
They also have relatively cheap labor, relatively
lax environmental rules compared to Europe

00:11:53.560 --> 00:11:54.560
or the US.

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:58.130
But to their credit, they also have lots of
engineering sophistication, especially in

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:03.220
electronics manufacturing at scale, which
is why they were able to develop, for example,

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:07.720
solar panels and transform the global solar
industry by bringing that to scale.

00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:11.390
The electric vehicle business, which is related
to lithium-ion batteries, but not the same

00:12:11.390 --> 00:12:16.130
of course, they have EVs at scale because
they're able to manufacture them and consume

00:12:16.130 --> 00:12:17.130
them.

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:21.540
And in the same way, they've been able to
do the world a service to some degree by producing

00:12:21.540 --> 00:12:26.269
lithium-ion batteries at massive scale, reducing
the costs so that everyone in the world can

00:12:26.269 --> 00:12:28.290
benefit from this technology.

00:12:28.290 --> 00:12:30.630
However, it does give them market power.

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:35.470
SIERRA: China’s dominance in the market
is a clear economic advantage.

00:12:35.470 --> 00:12:39.260
But some observers think it goes further than
that.

00:12:39.260 --> 00:12:46.750
SIERRA: What are the geopolitical risks then
that you see in terms of China's dominant

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:47.829
market position?

00:12:47.829 --> 00:12:52.410
FANNON: President Biden, from the steps of
Glasgow, during the climate conference of

00:12:52.410 --> 00:12:56.120
the parties, saw how oil prices were increasing.

00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:58.650
And it was creating challenges at home.

00:12:58.650 --> 00:13:02.620
No US driver wants to see high gas prices.

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:08.200
And so President Biden called on OPEC, to
increase production to lessen prices.

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:15.740
OPEC is a multi-country organization that
controls about 40% of global oil production.

00:13:15.740 --> 00:13:23.730
However, China controls anywhere from 50 to
90% of the minerals and the processing capability

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:25.550
for the clean energy transition.

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:31.360
So just the concept of reliance on one sole
supplier creates volatility in price.

00:13:31.360 --> 00:13:33.490
And that should be concerning for all of us.

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:38.300
Then if you put on top of that, that China
consistently is demonstrating itself to be

00:13:38.300 --> 00:13:42.670
America's and the West’s writ large, greatest
strategic threat.

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:49.100
This opens up a whole new area when we are
transitioning our economy from energy that

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:53.870
we have an abundance of, in the case of oil
and gas, to one in which we are in a deficit.

00:13:53.870 --> 00:13:58.600
And we would be extraordinarily reliant on
our greatest strategic threat.

00:13:58.600 --> 00:14:00.540
That should give us all pause.

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:06.830
SIERRA: So are there incidents from the past
that might illuminate how China could wield

00:14:06.830 --> 00:14:08.250
its market position?

00:14:08.250 --> 00:14:14.100
FANNON: China in the case of rare earth elements,
rare-earth elements is a grouping of elements

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:19.019
and metals, and minerals that are used in
magnets and different things, everything from

00:14:19.019 --> 00:14:20.410
wind turbines to electric vehicles.

00:14:20.410 --> 00:14:23.270
So they're crucial, but they're not a big
market.

00:14:23.270 --> 00:14:29.130
China became the world's leader in rare earth
production and processing, a lot of it because

00:14:29.130 --> 00:14:31.730
it's environmentally pretty nasty stuff.

00:14:31.730 --> 00:14:34.410
And China has a different standard set.

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:40.870
In 2010, China wielded its might in its dominance
of the rare earth supply chain, over Japan

00:14:40.870 --> 00:14:41.870
specifically.

00:14:41.870 --> 00:14:44.829
The two countries had been at odds over fishing
rights.

00:14:44.829 --> 00:14:49.680
Japan took one of the Chinese fishing vessels,
took the captain in.

00:14:49.680 --> 00:14:50.930
China didn't like it.

00:14:50.930 --> 00:14:54.870
And so they withheld rare earth element shipments
to Japan.

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:59.310
The prices globally went up over 300% in a
few days.

00:14:59.310 --> 00:15:04.910
So China has wielded its power and demonstrated
to the global market back in 2010.

00:15:04.910 --> 00:15:09.910
More recently, just a few years ago, during
the height of the US-China trade war as it

00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:10.910
was called.

00:15:10.910 --> 00:15:17.009
President Xi Jinping visited a rare earths
plant in China through state media, and the

00:15:17.009 --> 00:15:21.700
price jumped just then, and everyone was talking
about the possibility for a ban to the United

00:15:21.700 --> 00:15:22.700
States.

00:15:22.700 --> 00:15:27.990
That would completely stymie US and Western
security, as well as the clean energy transition.

00:15:27.990 --> 00:15:32.240
SIERRA: China's dominance in lithium is growing
in many ways at once.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:36.880
They own a lot of lithium deposits across
the world, and are buying up more.

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:38.019
They excel at processing.

00:15:38.019 --> 00:15:45.190
And to top it off, China also controls nearly
75% of all lithium battery manufacturing.

00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:52.510
As of 2020, it had 93 large scale lithium-ion
battery manufacturing plants, compared to

00:15:52.510 --> 00:15:54.610
just 4 in the US.

00:15:54.610 --> 00:16:00.010
VAITHEESWARAN: There's no doubt that having
an early leader advantage, first mover advantage,

00:16:00.010 --> 00:16:02.639
in an important industry of the future is
helpful.

00:16:02.639 --> 00:16:07.480
And you can see that again with the spillover
into their electric cars, which use lithium-ion

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:08.800
batteries for energy storage.

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:12.620
They're able to build up a supply chain tightly
integrated between those industries, the auto

00:16:12.620 --> 00:16:13.870
and energy storage industries.

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:17.010
And you can imagine other areas where they
could gain benefit.

00:16:17.010 --> 00:16:21.480
That's different from saying that it's some
kind of monopoly or cartel.

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:26.610
There's nothing stopping the United States
or Europe or other aspiring countries from

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.600
gaining competence and being really good at
manufacturing.

00:16:29.600 --> 00:16:33.579
There's nothing stopping them from entering
into long term contracts, for example, to

00:16:33.579 --> 00:16:37.910
develop resources of lithium from South America
or from Australia.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:42.110
And so if you could give long term certainty
and advanced market commitments to purchase

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:46.670
lots of lithium from these countries, there'd
be much more likely to provide the investment

00:16:46.670 --> 00:16:49.040
needed to develop their industry.

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:51.759
There's nothing the Chinese can do to stop
that.

00:16:51.759 --> 00:16:56.750
SIERRA: So with this long term thinking in
mind, is this sort of why China has been so

00:16:56.750 --> 00:16:59.920
successful at mining and refining lithium?

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:02.000
VAITHEESWARAN: There's a mix of factors.

00:17:02.000 --> 00:17:05.660
Chinese five year plans are longer term by
definition.

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:08.939
You know, we don't really do five year planning
in the US government or most other Western

00:17:08.939 --> 00:17:10.100
democracies.

00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:11.449
That doesn't mean they're always right.

00:17:11.449 --> 00:17:13.990
They make a lot of bets and a lot of them
don't work out.

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:18.310
But in a few strategic areas, at the moment
clean energy, biotechnology, and genomics,

00:17:18.310 --> 00:17:19.420
they're pretty well advertised.

00:17:19.420 --> 00:17:20.819
These are published reports.

00:17:20.819 --> 00:17:22.820
We can all read what the Chinese say they're
going to invest in.

00:17:22.820 --> 00:17:25.070
And they usually do invest in that area.

00:17:25.070 --> 00:17:27.420
And it tends to give a little bit more certainty
to market actors.

00:17:27.420 --> 00:17:33.090
But it also lines up the state banks that
provide subsidized aid or a nudge, nudge,

00:17:33.090 --> 00:17:37.730
wink, wink, soft loan to a favorite local
company that wants to get into a certain area.

00:17:37.730 --> 00:17:38.730
Why?

00:17:38.730 --> 00:17:40.820
Because the party has said it's a national
priority.

00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:44.640
Even if it doesn't work out, you'll be forgiven
for lending to a favored area.

00:17:44.640 --> 00:17:49.720
SIERRA: Lithium and the EV market spotlight
differences between the economic and political

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:52.040
systems in China and the US.

00:17:52.040 --> 00:17:57.990
China’s authoritarian system allows it to
set longer-term priorities and support companies

00:17:57.990 --> 00:18:02.280
financially, even if they need to run losses
to meet their strategic goals.

00:18:02.280 --> 00:18:08.520
VAITHEESWARAN: So you have this very, I would
say supportive network of both official as

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:12.679
well as unofficial ways of promoting some
of these technologies once the government

00:18:12.679 --> 00:18:15.790
has sent its signal that they're going to
be supported for a while.

00:18:15.790 --> 00:18:20.410
Whereas in the kind of Darwinian short-termist
capitalism that we tend to find in the US

00:18:20.410 --> 00:18:25.190
and UK and some other markets, a company that
makes the bet wrong or is too early to a market,

00:18:25.190 --> 00:18:29.440
if you come five years too early to an important
market, well that's as good as not coming

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:31.070
to the market at all or coming too late.

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:33.370
And your investors won't forgive you.

00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:38.350
SIERRA: It’s unclear how China might wield
its market power.

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:43.080
But regardless of what it does, many observers
believe that it’s time for the US and other

00:18:43.080 --> 00:18:49.100
countries to diversify the supply chain and
ramp up production in order to meet demand.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:53.790
VAITHEESWARAN: No company would typically
get reliant on one supply source or over overly

00:18:53.790 --> 00:18:54.790
reliant.

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:56.809
And in the same way, we should think more
broadly.

00:18:56.809 --> 00:19:01.140
And if there are things we can do at home
to remove obstacles to investment within America,

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:05.640
to help scale capital, to get demonstration
projects out of the laboratories and out of

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:10.440
the national energy labs and Department of
Energy and into the marketplace, then we should

00:19:10.440 --> 00:19:12.240
move much more aggressively on that.

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:15.990
And the soundings from the Biden administration
are this is what they want to do.

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:20.909
I think we're going to see this year several
things coming out of the administration, including

00:19:20.909 --> 00:19:25.270
international summits I think that'll be organized
around this to focus attention on this, to

00:19:25.270 --> 00:19:30.350
try to get a framework for reliable access,
maybe investment guarantees, maybe trying

00:19:30.350 --> 00:19:35.070
to depoliticize this issue so that we don't
end up with a situation where one country

00:19:35.070 --> 00:19:40.380
or another can bully others by guarding access
to resources, but rather have an international

00:19:40.380 --> 00:19:43.390
trade framework, a multilateral framework
of access.

00:19:43.390 --> 00:19:48.580
SIERRA: These frameworks might also take into
account labor conditions and human rights,

00:19:48.580 --> 00:19:54.299
especially in the Global South where a great
deal of lithium resources are currently extracted.

00:19:54.299 --> 00:19:59.929
FANNON: A lot of these countries, and particularly
in the developing world, are increasingly

00:19:59.929 --> 00:20:03.120
concerned about doing business with China.

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:07.720
They're desperate for alternatives to find
non-Chinese investment.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:11.500
Everyone knows about the Belt and Road Initiative,
where they make investments and it's kind

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:12.740
of rapacious.

00:20:12.740 --> 00:20:16.270
They've pursued a similar kind of approach
when it comes to mining where it's just to

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:18.270
extract the minerals.

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:21.620
There's no investment in the country, no support
of the local community.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:27.220
And it's really a shovel and ship mentality,
where they just come in, take, and then have

00:20:27.220 --> 00:20:30.320
all the value accretion done in China.

00:20:30.320 --> 00:20:35.240
So the country is actually left in a worse
place than ever before.

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:41.059
You know, when it comes to oil, there is a
concept of the oil curse or the resource curse,

00:20:41.059 --> 00:20:46.770
where a country that is oil rich actually
ends up becoming worse off after the exploitation

00:20:46.770 --> 00:20:47.770
of oil.

00:20:47.770 --> 00:20:50.820
All of that has to do with the linkage to
corruption.

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:56.050
China is pursuing a policy that's very much
aligned with that, but focused on the minerals

00:20:56.050 --> 00:20:57.050
for clean energy.

00:20:57.050 --> 00:20:58.690
It's the same kind of playbook.

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:04.630
SIERRA: If the US became more active in the
lithium supply chain, it would have the opportunity

00:21:04.630 --> 00:21:08.970
to invest in a more equitable way, especially
at home.

00:21:08.970 --> 00:21:12.300
And that brings us to an option that we haven’t
talked about yet.

00:21:12.300 --> 00:21:18.070
SIERRA: Do you think that we can mine and
refine our own lithium here in the US?

00:21:18.070 --> 00:21:19.590
I mean, should we?

00:21:19.590 --> 00:21:21.100
FANNON: Absolutely, we should.

00:21:21.100 --> 00:21:22.529
And we have to.

00:21:22.529 --> 00:21:25.540
To achieve our climate targets, we have to
find new sources.

00:21:25.540 --> 00:21:30.580
And as I mentioned, the US has abundant lithium
opportunities, as well as Canada.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:36.550
So yes, we have it, but what we've not done
is reconciled the license to operate.

00:21:36.550 --> 00:21:42.279
It takes, according to the International Energy
Agency, up to 16 years on average, to go from

00:21:42.279 --> 00:21:45.419
a mine discovery to production.

00:21:45.419 --> 00:21:50.310
So if you think about that, the Paris Climate
Accord was only about six years ago.

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:54.700
We haven't really started discovering this
stuff and developing the mines, and then the

00:21:54.700 --> 00:21:56.480
processing capabilities.

00:21:56.480 --> 00:21:58.679
So that's absolutely critically important.

00:21:58.679 --> 00:22:00.870
And we have to find new ways to do it.

00:22:00.870 --> 00:22:03.220
We have to speed it up.

00:22:03.220 --> 00:22:08.720
VAITHEESWARAN: There's no insurmountable barrier
in the US to America becoming a clean energy

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:16.220
powerhouse that utilizes lithium, makes lithium-ion
batteries and ultimately is able to take advantage

00:22:16.220 --> 00:22:18.970
of the properties that lithium makes possible.

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:23.630
Now, what barriers we have, some of them have
to do with the nature of our capitalist markets.

00:22:23.630 --> 00:22:26.650
A second area of course is nimbyism.

00:22:26.650 --> 00:22:32.690
Lots of people don't like to have mines developed
in their areas, native lands, or even on federal

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:33.690
lands.

00:22:33.690 --> 00:22:36.309
There's a lot of obstruction and there is
some water pollution that results from mining.

00:22:36.309 --> 00:22:41.429
And so are we going to insist on higher environmental
standards or help the latest technologies

00:22:41.429 --> 00:22:45.799
for environmental remediation be applied from
day one to give reassurance to communities

00:22:45.799 --> 00:22:49.779
that this is not going to be just another
toxic mess we're going to leave in your backyard

00:22:49.779 --> 00:22:53.480
and someone somewhere else will be driving
a fancy electric car thinking that they're

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:54.770
being very green.

00:22:54.770 --> 00:22:56.890
So getting that right, and we're not there
yet.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:58.390
I think that would help.

00:22:58.390 --> 00:23:01.809
We're never going to be as cheap and cheerful
as China because frankly they run roughshod

00:23:01.809 --> 00:23:06.530
over local communities, over capital markets,
over environmental concerns, over labor concerns.

00:23:06.530 --> 00:23:10.750
I don't think we want to be that country when
it comes to making clean energy work.

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:12.820
But I think we can do it at a higher quality
level.

00:23:12.820 --> 00:23:16.420
And we might see Europe as for example our
competitor or frenemy in this.

00:23:16.420 --> 00:23:20.620
Europe is forging ahead pretty quickly in
this area and we might be able to learn some

00:23:20.620 --> 00:23:22.850
lessons from the way that Europe is working
on this.

00:23:22.850 --> 00:23:27.330
SIERRA: Well, it feels like such a funny cyclical
sort of argument.

00:23:27.330 --> 00:23:33.080
Right, where, like, if I was being told that
there was going to be a lithium mine in my

00:23:33.080 --> 00:23:37.030
backyard, I'd be, not too happy about it.

00:23:37.030 --> 00:23:42.560
But then the argument is that, if we have
the lithium, we can use it for clean energy.

00:23:42.560 --> 00:23:44.470
How do you reconcile that kind of conflict?

00:23:44.470 --> 00:23:46.030
FANNON: You're right.

00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:48.580
No one wants to have a mine in their backyard.

00:23:48.580 --> 00:23:50.690
That's completely reasonable.

00:23:50.690 --> 00:23:55.930
But every clean energy technology, however
clean and green it might be, requires a very

00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:59.330
big shovel at the beginning of its life cycle.

00:23:59.330 --> 00:24:03.180
Those shovels have to be deployed, or we're
not going to realize that clean energy future.

00:24:03.180 --> 00:24:07.940
And, I would say most of all of us want to
address climate change in some way.

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:14.169
But operationalizing that is much harder than
just speaking about a big concept.

00:24:14.169 --> 00:24:17.650
Environmentalists as well as certain governments
want clean energy.

00:24:17.650 --> 00:24:19.510
But they're challenged on how to execute.

00:24:19.510 --> 00:24:25.580
Again, the scale of what we're talking about,
500% increase in demand for these metals,

00:24:25.580 --> 00:24:28.260
the trillions of dollars required in investment.

00:24:28.260 --> 00:24:33.039
If you can't build something because someone
opposes it, however well intentioned some

00:24:33.039 --> 00:24:37.020
local environmentalist might be, then you're
not going to make that investment.

00:24:37.020 --> 00:24:43.299
I think that a certain segment of the electric
vehicle purchaser is buying that vehicle because

00:24:43.299 --> 00:24:48.550
they want a product that doesn't pollute,
and that doesn't emit greenhouse gas emissions.

00:24:48.550 --> 00:24:54.169
I think having that same consumer, be empowered
to understand where their supply chain comes

00:24:54.169 --> 00:24:55.169
from.

00:24:55.169 --> 00:25:00.039
And also to compare the way producing that
lithium in America would look, and processing

00:25:00.039 --> 00:25:02.830
that would look, compared to how it's currently
being done.

00:25:02.830 --> 00:25:06.529
I think this is the kind of awakening that
we all need.

00:25:06.529 --> 00:25:09.760
And that starts really in your backyard.

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:14.790
We have to go from a NIMBY, Not In My BackYard,
to a YIMBY situation.

00:25:14.790 --> 00:25:19.870
And this requires taking on very challenging
political constituencies at home.

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:22.429
SIERRA: Is that a yes in my backyard?

00:25:22.429 --> 00:25:24.750
FANNON: Yes in my backyard.

00:25:24.750 --> 00:25:26.080
Yes.

00:25:26.080 --> 00:25:33.110
SIERRA: One of the first catalysts for climate
action was Al Gore’s documentary An Inconvenient

00:25:33.110 --> 00:25:34.110
Truth.

00:25:34.110 --> 00:25:38.380
At the time, the main challenge seemed to
be convincing people that climate change was

00:25:38.380 --> 00:25:39.390
real and man-made.

00:25:39.390 --> 00:25:42.630
SIERRA: The challenge has evolved.

00:25:42.630 --> 00:25:47.049
Now that there’s growing recognition about
the nature of the problem, focus is shifting

00:25:47.049 --> 00:25:52.470
to the question of how we actually alter the
infrastructure of our world.

00:25:52.470 --> 00:25:56.650
As this happens, there will be a lot more
inconveniences along the way.

00:25:56.650 --> 00:26:01.490
SIERRA: This new world of ours needs batteries,
and a lot more lithium.

00:26:01.490 --> 00:26:05.520
If we are going to succeed, someone is going
to produce that lithium.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:10.740
Sure, China is currently ahead, and there
are a lot of challenges, but the question

00:26:10.740 --> 00:26:22.080
of who will lead this transition is still
up for grabs.

